16
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:37,671
[SPEAKER_01]: President, for present three absent, the meeting is called to order.

17
00:00:38,392 --> 00:00:43,533
[SPEAKER_01]: There will be a meeting of the Medford City Council Committee of the Whole on Wednesday, May 18th, 2022 at 6 p.m.

18
00:00:43,573 --> 00:00:44,094
[SPEAKER_01]: via Zoom.

19
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:55,137
[SPEAKER_01]: The purpose of the meeting is to review and propose draft language to establish an ordinance around leaf blower use within the city in the interest of public health and safety for Medford residents, paper 21-057.

20
00:00:55,537 --> 00:00:59,979
[SPEAKER_01]: DPW Commissioner Tim McGibbon and KP Law Attorney Janelle Austin have been invited to attend.

21
00:01:00,539 --> 00:01:03,742
[SPEAKER_01]: For further information aids and accommodations contact the city clerk at 781-393-2425.

22
00:01:03,782 --> 00:01:10,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So we met the subcommittee for climate sustainability and transportation met on this paper, about a month ago.

23
00:01:16,915 --> 00:01:19,758
[SPEAKER_01]: We have the draft ordinance from KP law that we reviewed.

24
00:01:19,778 --> 00:01:21,419
[SPEAKER_01]: I can share that on my screen.

25
00:01:22,099 --> 00:01:23,881
[SPEAKER_01]: We did not have any formal motions.

26
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:34,069
[SPEAKER_01]: We and the subcommittee felt that it was in a position that we wanted to move forward that draft ordinance to get feedback from the full council and any motions to adjust it there.

27
00:01:34,509 --> 00:01:42,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And we did have building commissioner Moki join us last time to just give us some insight as far as enforcement around this.

28
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:45,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And he does believe it is something that they have the,

29
00:01:46,299 --> 00:01:48,041
[SPEAKER_01]: with people power to enforce at this time.

30
00:01:48,481 --> 00:02:01,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So I left him off this meeting, but I'm always happy to circle him in for more, any feedback or have him come to a future meeting, but I figured since we already got that information from him, we didn't have to take another night away from him.

31
00:02:02,162 --> 00:02:07,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And now we do have KP Law Attorney Janelle Austin, as well as DPW Commissioner Tim McGivern with us.

32
00:02:08,323 --> 00:02:22,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Scarpelli raised last time a number of questions that he felt that DPW Commissioner McGivern may be able to answer for us around battery usage and just kind of commonplace usage within the city departments as far as leaf blowers.

33
00:02:23,106 --> 00:02:30,547
[SPEAKER_01]: So unless other councilors would like to start with discussion or questions, I'm happy to share my screen and kind of go through

34
00:02:31,287 --> 00:02:35,571
[SPEAKER_01]: The ordinance language and just highlight the salient points as we've had this in our inbox.

35
00:02:36,331 --> 00:02:38,593
[SPEAKER_01]: I think since last month and was sent around again today.

36
00:02:43,357 --> 00:02:44,458
[SPEAKER_09]: And I will share my screen then.

37
00:02:56,928 --> 00:02:58,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So before we have the draft ordinance,

38
00:03:00,815 --> 00:03:11,645
[SPEAKER_01]: There was some questions at the subcommittee just in this definitions portion, I think, particularly as far as the commercial leaf blower operator as there are some solo.

39
00:03:13,047 --> 00:03:22,516
[SPEAKER_01]: There are some kind of one man band operations that may be subject to these regulations, if we wanted to adjust that from a two person two person plus organization to.

40
00:03:23,353 --> 00:03:25,915
[SPEAKER_01]: anyone running a commercial operation.

41
00:03:27,036 --> 00:03:38,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Leaf blowers does reference, include both battery and electric powered, I'm sorry, gas, battery and electric powered leaf blowers as far as the language of this ordinance goes.

42
00:03:39,478 --> 00:03:56,010
[SPEAKER_01]: And the main limitations around use reference are noise ordinance that already exists, as well as limitations that leaf blowers, use of leaf blowers is prohibited except for between March 15th and May 31st and October 1st and December 15th in any year.

43
00:03:57,051 --> 00:04:02,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It also exempts city parks and a number of city properties from these regulations.

44
00:04:04,326 --> 00:04:09,550
[SPEAKER_01]: It further prohibits the use of leaf blowers except between the hours of nine to five Monday through Saturday.

45
00:04:09,570 --> 00:04:13,513
[SPEAKER_01]: And it also prohibits the use on holidays.

46
00:04:14,914 --> 00:04:17,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, the city properties are exempt from this.

47
00:04:19,118 --> 00:04:21,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Then section three requests that

48
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:33,164
[SPEAKER_01]: those who are either owners of large parcels, which is a small number for Medford, or have the commercial operation to register with the city and provide a plan.

49
00:04:34,584 --> 00:04:47,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also just some general language around use as far as leaf blower operations shall not cause leaves, dirt, dust, rodent, dog or cat feces, debris, glass clippings, cuttings or trimmings from trees or shrubs or any other type to litter or debris.

50
00:04:48,343 --> 00:04:52,548
[SPEAKER_01]: to be blown or deposited on any adjacent property or any other parcel of land.

51
00:04:53,543 --> 00:04:59,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So just mitigating that, you know, the actual blowing of things on other properties that may be detrimental.

52
00:05:00,044 --> 00:05:11,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also going into referencing the decibels once again, as well as number six on parcels of 10,000 square feet or less, only one leaf blower at a time may be used.

53
00:05:12,367 --> 00:05:18,248
[SPEAKER_01]: And then on parcels larger than 10,000 square feet, only one leaf blower may be used within each 10,000 square foot area.

54
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:32,394
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also the limitation set forth in the to be numbered subsections shall not apply to de minimis use of a leaf blower by an individual on the property in which they reside, provided that such use is in compliance with the noise ordinance.

55
00:05:32,514 --> 00:05:36,535
[SPEAKER_01]: For the subsection de minimis shall mean not more than an aggregate of five minutes per day.

56
00:05:36,555 --> 00:05:40,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It references the enforcement authorities.

57
00:05:40,576 --> 00:05:47,918
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, Commissioner Mokey was fine with the code enforcement building department being the enforcement authority and did recommend

58
00:05:48,951 --> 00:05:52,453
[SPEAKER_01]: seeing if the health department would work in concert as well.

59
00:05:53,434 --> 00:05:55,155
[SPEAKER_01]: So we could also loop them in at a future date.

60
00:05:56,075 --> 00:06:01,079
[SPEAKER_01]: And so those are the general overview of the health department.

61
00:06:02,448 --> 00:06:03,769
[SPEAKER_01]: what this ordinance entails.

62
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:06,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, we didn't make any motions at the subcommittee.

63
00:06:06,671 --> 00:06:09,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, we did have some questions just regarding the definitions.

64
00:06:10,374 --> 00:06:19,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And there was some discussion among residents as far as the duration for which leaf lowers would be permitted to be used.

65
00:06:19,962 --> 00:06:25,707
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm happy to open it up to questions from counselors at this point.

66
00:06:26,727 --> 00:06:27,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Collins.

67
00:06:29,745 --> 00:06:31,466
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you, President Morrell for that overview.

68
00:06:31,486 --> 00:06:40,493
[SPEAKER_02]: I do have some more substantial motions that I'd like to discuss later in the meeting, but for now, I just had a couple of clarifying questions about this draft.

69
00:06:40,653 --> 00:06:43,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Going back to that section, I think it's section seven on de minimis use.

70
00:06:43,976 --> 00:06:47,578
[SPEAKER_02]: I just wanted to make sure I understood what this section was referring to.

71
00:06:47,638 --> 00:06:58,767
[SPEAKER_02]: Does that mean if a leaf blower is used on a property very minimally, so for less than five minutes per day, then it's not subject to the same noise regulations and seasonal regulations?

72
00:06:59,944 --> 00:07:01,185
[SPEAKER_01]: That was my understanding.

73
00:07:01,465 --> 00:07:03,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Attorney Austin, if you're able to speak to that.

74
00:07:03,667 --> 00:07:07,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Council President Morelle.

75
00:07:07,270 --> 00:07:08,431
[SPEAKER_00]: That is correct.

76
00:07:08,471 --> 00:07:15,336
[SPEAKER_00]: It would basically exempt individualized use for a very narrow specific timeframe.

77
00:07:15,996 --> 00:07:22,541
[SPEAKER_00]: And certainly that can be adjusted if the council feels as a matter of policy, it wishes to make the adjustment.

78
00:07:22,601 --> 00:07:27,085
[SPEAKER_00]: This is consistent with other communities and how they have regulated the use

79
00:07:27,785 --> 00:07:35,951
[SPEAKER_00]: you know, across the state to allow individuals to use it on the property themselves for non-commercial use, Councilor Collins.

80
00:07:36,011 --> 00:07:36,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you.

81
00:07:38,613 --> 00:07:38,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Great.

82
00:07:39,273 --> 00:07:41,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you so much for that confirmation.

83
00:07:42,716 --> 00:07:45,418
[SPEAKER_02]: And then one other clarifying question, if I may.

84
00:07:46,298 --> 00:07:55,305
[SPEAKER_02]: Jumping up to, I want to say, section B3, I was just hoping for a little bit more clarification on the permitting process and what that

85
00:07:56,158 --> 00:07:58,779
[SPEAKER_02]: what that would create.

86
00:07:58,819 --> 00:08:11,325
[SPEAKER_02]: Does that mean that if a commercial operator or the owner of a large property submits and gets approved this mitigation plan, in that case, they are allowed to go outside the seasonal windows?

87
00:08:11,425 --> 00:08:13,086
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that what this section outlines?

88
00:08:16,228 --> 00:08:17,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Attorney Austin, if you're able to speak to that.

89
00:08:18,509 --> 00:08:18,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

90
00:08:20,009 --> 00:08:24,272
[SPEAKER_00]: In my opinion, that subsection three provision would exempt

91
00:08:25,810 --> 00:08:38,577
[SPEAKER_00]: exempt if there is a permit or permission given, it would not, the prohibition would then not apply given that the entity has gone through the permitting process with the approval of the city.

92
00:08:38,717 --> 00:08:39,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So yes, it would exempt.

93
00:08:42,019 --> 00:08:48,163
[SPEAKER_00]: As long as there was a valid operations landline record with the city that was approved by the building commissioner or designee.

94
00:08:48,183 --> 00:08:51,865
[SPEAKER_02]: Great, thank you again for confirming.

95
00:08:52,505 --> 00:08:53,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Thank you, Councilor Collins.

96
00:08:56,932 --> 00:08:59,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Any other questions from counselors?

97
00:09:00,713 --> 00:09:03,635
[SPEAKER_01]: If I actually just could, I just have a follow-up question to that, Attorney Austin.

98
00:09:03,675 --> 00:09:11,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So would it then be that the idea is that these plans would be submitted and that would cover an entire season of use?

99
00:09:11,197 --> 00:09:15,039
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be a single one-off use under Section B3?

100
00:09:19,018 --> 00:09:29,284
[SPEAKER_00]: It could be done on a, I think that that provision contemplates that regulations will be adopted and certainly it could be a seasonal or yearly basis.

101
00:09:29,965 --> 00:09:43,633
[SPEAKER_00]: I would suspect, but certainly defer to the council in terms of policy, that it may be done on an annualized basis in light of the fact that things may change and equipment may change versus a season.

102
00:09:43,673 --> 00:09:46,655
[SPEAKER_00]: A season might be, you know, fairly onerous in terms of

103
00:09:47,540 --> 00:09:52,181
[SPEAKER_00]: the time and commitment required by city employees and officials to review the operations plan.

104
00:09:52,521 --> 00:09:57,443
[SPEAKER_00]: But certainly that could be addressed or clarified if the council would like through regulations.

105
00:10:01,844 --> 00:10:02,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Attorney Austin.

106
00:10:04,465 --> 00:10:05,605
[SPEAKER_09]: Any Councillor Seng?

107
00:10:13,106 --> 00:10:14,427
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you.

108
00:10:14,907 --> 00:10:16,948
[SPEAKER_06]: I had a question for Commissioner McGivern.

109
00:10:18,669 --> 00:10:24,511
[SPEAKER_06]: One of the policy questions that we have to look at as a council is looking at the dates, whether you want to shorten them.

110
00:10:25,812 --> 00:10:31,194
[SPEAKER_06]: And I thought you might be able to give us an idea as to when the city does leaf cleanups.

111
00:10:31,635 --> 00:10:40,679
[SPEAKER_06]: Are there certain weeks approximately that you would say are much more intense or much more focused on leaf cleanups?

112
00:10:50,538 --> 00:10:52,500
[SPEAKER_04]: Hello, thank you, can you hear me?

113
00:10:53,321 --> 00:10:53,621
Yes.

114
00:10:54,001 --> 00:10:54,922
[SPEAKER_04]: Excellent, hello.

115
00:10:54,942 --> 00:10:57,824
[SPEAKER_04]: Hello, councilors, hello, President Morrell.

116
00:10:58,385 --> 00:11:02,048
[SPEAKER_04]: So what do you have there for a range in the ordinance as written?

117
00:11:05,391 --> 00:11:07,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Right now, let me scroll up.

118
00:11:09,715 --> 00:11:13,839
[SPEAKER_01]: It is March 15th through May 31st and October 1st through December 15th.

119
00:11:22,288 --> 00:11:23,789
[SPEAKER_04]: those dates pretty much nail it.

120
00:11:24,069 --> 00:11:28,013
[SPEAKER_04]: So for example, you know, we just got finished with our big spring cleanup.

121
00:11:28,894 --> 00:11:30,595
[SPEAKER_04]: So just last week.

122
00:11:31,196 --> 00:11:34,299
[SPEAKER_04]: So that fits in that first timeframe.

123
00:11:34,339 --> 00:11:42,226
[SPEAKER_04]: And then in the fall, I don't know off the top of my head, but I believe November, early November is our big sweep.

124
00:11:42,506 --> 00:11:43,507
[SPEAKER_04]: So we'll try to clean up the,

125
00:11:44,343 --> 00:11:51,068
[SPEAKER_04]: the leaves there, but the timeframe of October to December 13th, yeah, that would be Primo leaf cleanup time.

126
00:11:51,308 --> 00:11:56,611
[SPEAKER_04]: So I don't think, unless you want to narrow these down, I think these ranges are appropriate.

127
00:11:57,012 --> 00:12:03,436
[SPEAKER_04]: From our operations standpoint, I can get you detailed information, but I can tell you, we just finished a big spring cleanup.

128
00:12:05,097 --> 00:12:05,438
[SPEAKER_06]: Thank you.

129
00:12:05,758 --> 00:12:10,081
[SPEAKER_06]: I would be curious as to, I mean, if this is reasonable,

130
00:12:10,843 --> 00:12:11,844
[SPEAKER_06]: fully happy to accept it.

131
00:12:12,984 --> 00:12:16,606
[SPEAKER_06]: I know there is some desire for us to maybe shorten it a little bit.

132
00:12:18,247 --> 00:12:23,349
[SPEAKER_06]: If you could provide us with a more specific picture of what that would look like, that'd be great for us.

133
00:12:23,429 --> 00:12:30,933
[SPEAKER_06]: But if you have an idea of what shortening it might look like and still not be too disruptive, that might be helpful.

134
00:12:31,433 --> 00:12:32,674
[SPEAKER_06]: But yeah.

135
00:12:33,274 --> 00:12:39,197
[SPEAKER_04]: And the application is for a commercial leaf blower use basically throughout the city, right?

136
00:12:39,738 --> 00:12:40,278
[SPEAKER_04]: I believe so.

137
00:12:40,968 --> 00:12:56,435
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, yeah, I'll provide the council through the president sort of what DPW's prime date ranges for leaf cleanup is, and that'll help maybe confirm this target.

138
00:12:57,395 --> 00:12:58,976
[SPEAKER_08]: Thank you, that would be super helpful.

139
00:12:59,676 --> 00:13:04,698
[SPEAKER_08]: Absolutely.

140
00:13:04,718 --> 00:13:05,859
[SPEAKER_09]: What's that, Councilor Sang?

141
00:13:07,619 --> 00:13:08,720
[SPEAKER_02]: Great, Councilor Collins.

142
00:13:11,671 --> 00:13:12,613
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you, President Morrell.

143
00:13:13,374 --> 00:13:16,519
[SPEAKER_02]: And thank you, Commissioner McGovern for speaking to that.

144
00:13:16,559 --> 00:13:18,182
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are some questions that I had as well.

145
00:13:20,543 --> 00:13:31,250
[SPEAKER_02]: So yeah, I wanted to move on from some of my clarifying questions on the ordinance to sort of considering it a bit more broadly, a bit more sort of the opportunity this ordinance presents.

146
00:13:33,592 --> 00:13:40,016
[SPEAKER_02]: I love the regulations that this ordinance lays out regarding seasonal effective dates, noise regulation.

147
00:13:40,056 --> 00:13:44,219
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that these are issues that I personally have heard from a lot of constituents about.

148
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:48,802
[SPEAKER_02]: I also think that this is an opportunity to consider

149
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:52,862
[SPEAKER_02]: phasing out the use of gas-powered leaf blowers entirely.

150
00:13:53,102 --> 00:14:11,071
[SPEAKER_02]: So something that I've heard from several constituents about while this was working its way through the subcommittee, the environmental impacts of gas-powered leaf blowers are extensively documented, local air pollution, creation of ozone, all these factors that exacerbate climate change.

151
00:14:11,091 --> 00:14:14,353
[SPEAKER_02]: I was reading something that said that sort of pound for pound,

152
00:14:14,810 --> 00:14:18,012
[SPEAKER_02]: these gas powered leaf blowers are more polluting than cars even.

153
00:14:18,613 --> 00:14:24,597
[SPEAKER_02]: So I think that this poses a chance for us to be a real, a leader on mitigating the causes of climate change.

154
00:14:25,858 --> 00:14:42,390
[SPEAKER_02]: And so I'd love for us to discuss if this could be an opportunity to phase out their use entirely, you know, considering the polystyrene ban ordinance that this council recently discussed, you know, similar to that, I think that there's a way that we could do this in a way that gives property owners

155
00:14:43,018 --> 00:14:53,127
[SPEAKER_02]: commercial operators ample time to prepare for phasing out, you know, perhaps even an opportunity to defer effective date again like we did with the polystyrene ban.

156
00:14:53,147 --> 00:15:01,755
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, with the ongoing supply chain issues which I'm sure is affecting the lawn care supplies industry as it is almost every other industry.

157
00:15:03,988 --> 00:15:13,215
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that this is something that for environmental and public health reasons, I'd love for this council to consider to make this ordinance even stronger, even more protective of public health.

158
00:15:13,936 --> 00:15:22,222
[SPEAKER_02]: And at the same time, balance that with not forcing property owners and commercial operators to turn on a dime and have their business impacted.

159
00:15:23,443 --> 00:15:33,571
[SPEAKER_02]: So for that reason, I'd like to make the motion to have our council provide draft language on a phased in prohibition

160
00:15:34,788 --> 00:15:37,909
[SPEAKER_02]: on gas-powered leaf blowers to add to this ordinance.

161
00:15:41,629 --> 00:15:42,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Councilor Collins.

162
00:15:42,410 --> 00:15:43,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a second on that motion?

163
00:15:43,790 --> 00:15:52,731
[SPEAKER_05]: I would second it if the Councilor would be amenable to amending it, that the phase-in would occur over two years.

164
00:15:53,552 --> 00:15:55,152
[SPEAKER_01]: That was exactly what I had in mind.

165
00:15:56,312 --> 00:15:57,672
[SPEAKER_05]: Great, then I second it.

166
00:15:59,453 --> 00:16:00,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Councilor Collins.

167
00:16:01,113 --> 00:16:01,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Knight.

168
00:16:02,938 --> 00:16:04,139
[SPEAKER_03]: Madam President, thank you very much.

169
00:16:04,799 --> 00:16:10,263
[SPEAKER_03]: Point of notice would be that the City of Medford's Parks Department is responsible for over 118 acres of land.

170
00:16:10,683 --> 00:16:12,364
[SPEAKER_03]: This doesn't include the school department's lands.

171
00:16:13,145 --> 00:16:14,245
[SPEAKER_03]: Let's put our money where our mouth is.

172
00:16:14,265 --> 00:16:18,908
[SPEAKER_03]: Why are we going to hold the commercial establishments to a higher standard than that we're going to hold ourselves to?

173
00:16:20,870 --> 00:16:21,330
[SPEAKER_03]: Number one.

174
00:16:21,990 --> 00:16:23,691
[SPEAKER_03]: Number two, what are we going to ban next?

175
00:16:24,492 --> 00:16:27,894
[SPEAKER_03]: I think that we're really going a little ban-crazy lately.

176
00:16:28,074 --> 00:16:31,356
[SPEAKER_03]: Regulate, maybe, but not ban would be a better way to do this, in my opinion.

177
00:16:33,093 --> 00:16:36,155
[SPEAKER_03]: So those are a couple of thoughts that I have about this.

178
00:16:36,295 --> 00:16:41,198
[SPEAKER_03]: But ultimately, I was just hoping you could read the intent of the ordinance back.

179
00:16:41,879 --> 00:16:43,500
[SPEAKER_03]: That's usually the preamble.

180
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:47,462
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't mean to say the intended purpose of the ordinance, just to read that back.

181
00:16:47,542 --> 00:16:53,246
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, we have

182
00:16:57,103 --> 00:16:58,444
[SPEAKER_01]: The leaf blower is regulated.

183
00:16:58,484 --> 00:17:05,487
[SPEAKER_01]: We have the definitions, limitations on use, and enforcement regulation, severability, and effective date.

184
00:17:05,547 --> 00:17:08,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So I think we could use language on intent.

185
00:17:09,690 --> 00:17:11,571
[SPEAKER_03]: I think that that would be appropriate, Madam President.

186
00:17:12,811 --> 00:17:25,418
[SPEAKER_03]: Also, I'd like to offer the motion that anybody that has a registered business that provides landscaping services be invited to a meeting, because most people that I talk to that are landscapers in this community have no idea about this proposal.

187
00:17:26,421 --> 00:17:31,145
[SPEAKER_03]: And most of the time, especially this time of year, they're not sitting in the TV in the living room, they're out working.

188
00:17:31,806 --> 00:17:41,914
[SPEAKER_03]: So with the good weather upon us and this season upon us, it might make sense for us to invite some of these individuals that are actually going to be impacted and affected monetarily by this.

189
00:17:44,023 --> 00:17:49,408
[SPEAKER_03]: proposal, number one, and number two, also maybe hear from some of our friends from the Chamber of Commerce as to what they think.

190
00:17:50,029 --> 00:17:51,590
[SPEAKER_03]: Ultimately, we know what's going to happen, right?

191
00:17:51,610 --> 00:17:53,312
[SPEAKER_03]: The cost is just going to get passed on to the consumer.

192
00:17:53,412 --> 00:18:01,080
[SPEAKER_03]: So it might be interesting to hear what impact this is going to have on the consumer from the business owners as this proposal is anticipated through the vetting process.

193
00:18:03,642 --> 00:18:07,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And do you want the first as far as adding, making sure the intent is included.

194
00:18:07,323 --> 00:18:13,366
[SPEAKER_03]: Do you want that in a motion or just a recommendation to put that in the motion in the form of motion or as a recommendation committee report.

195
00:18:13,406 --> 00:18:16,107
[SPEAKER_03]: However, you guys said, I don't think it's going to be something that's too contentious.

196
00:18:17,147 --> 00:18:21,609
[SPEAKER_07]: So I didn't get all the language about the, about the recommendation.

197
00:18:24,050 --> 00:18:28,211
[SPEAKER_01]: The recommendation is just to, to add the intent portion for the ordinance.

198
00:18:28,531 --> 00:18:28,811
[SPEAKER_07]: Okay.

199
00:18:28,891 --> 00:18:29,172
[SPEAKER_07]: Got it.

200
00:18:29,372 --> 00:18:29,592
[SPEAKER_07]: Got it.

201
00:18:29,712 --> 00:18:29,992
[SPEAKER_07]: Got that.

202
00:18:32,691 --> 00:18:39,955
[SPEAKER_01]: And do I have a second on Councilor Knight's motion to invite all those registered landscaping companies within the city as well as the Chamber of Commerce.

203
00:18:40,475 --> 00:18:41,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Second by Councilor Collins.

204
00:18:42,756 --> 00:18:44,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

205
00:18:44,857 --> 00:18:46,178
[SPEAKER_01]: We can take all motions at the end.

206
00:18:49,860 --> 00:18:50,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Vice President Behrs.

207
00:18:55,003 --> 00:18:55,963
[SPEAKER_05]: Was Councilor Knight finished?

208
00:18:56,824 --> 00:18:57,344
[SPEAKER_01]: I apologize.

209
00:18:57,364 --> 00:18:59,385
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought, did you have anything more Councilor Knight?

210
00:19:02,813 --> 00:19:03,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

211
00:19:03,554 --> 00:19:04,054
[SPEAKER_03]: Thank you.

212
00:19:04,154 --> 00:19:06,476
[SPEAKER_03]: I said, I said, thank you very much, Madam President.

213
00:19:06,556 --> 00:19:06,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry.

214
00:19:07,437 --> 00:19:07,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

215
00:19:07,897 --> 00:19:08,678
[SPEAKER_03]: That was a short one.

216
00:19:08,698 --> 00:19:09,659
[SPEAKER_03]: That was a short one for me.

217
00:19:09,679 --> 00:19:09,959
[SPEAKER_03]: I know.

218
00:19:12,040 --> 00:19:12,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Vice President Behrs.

219
00:19:13,622 --> 00:19:14,682
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Madam President.

220
00:19:17,104 --> 00:19:26,972
[SPEAKER_05]: I had kind of five items relevant to this relative to this draft, maybe a sixth after further reading during this meeting.

221
00:19:28,033 --> 00:19:31,896
[SPEAKER_05]: One was, I think there should be a prompt and permanent phase out of gas powered leaf blowers.

222
00:19:33,366 --> 00:19:39,030
[SPEAKER_05]: and I think we now have a motion and a second to get some draft language around that.

223
00:19:40,291 --> 00:19:45,075
[SPEAKER_05]: And I do know that we were already holding the city to that standard and I think we should hold everyone else to that same standard.

224
00:19:47,997 --> 00:19:53,862
[SPEAKER_05]: Restrictions on the time of day, I think that makes sense as well, nine to five, which is currently written here.

225
00:19:55,043 --> 00:20:00,807
[SPEAKER_05]: In terms of the times of year, I'm okay with the ones that are in here, although I do think that December 1st to December 15th

226
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:10,945
[SPEAKER_05]: is generally a little bit late in the season for leaf removal, at least where we are in North Medford.

227
00:20:11,005 --> 00:20:18,148
[SPEAKER_05]: But I'd be open to a discussion around that, especially maybe a little bit of a shortening of the fall end of it.

228
00:20:20,665 --> 00:20:36,797
[SPEAKER_05]: One thing that I saw, you know, I think we got it pretty close to right on restricting how many leaf blowers can be running at the same time on a single property, but I didn't see addressed was how long they can be constantly making noise on a single property.

229
00:20:38,018 --> 00:20:47,506
[SPEAKER_05]: I'd like to make a motion that we have language to limit them from consistently being on for more than one hour per day.

230
00:20:47,526 --> 00:20:48,086
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, so if,

231
00:20:49,803 --> 00:20:58,348
[SPEAKER_05]: someone uses it for 15 minutes here, 10 minutes there, five minutes there, that would be different.

232
00:20:58,368 --> 00:21:06,252
[SPEAKER_05]: But I think one of the issues is just when it's the constant droning and noise for an hour or an hour and a half, that that's very disruptive.

233
00:21:06,412 --> 00:21:14,596
[SPEAKER_05]: So the motion would be to limit constant use to one hour per day per property.

234
00:21:14,616 --> 00:21:17,518
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

235
00:21:18,099 --> 00:21:19,200
[SPEAKER_03]: Point of information, Madam President.

236
00:21:19,961 --> 00:21:21,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Point of information, Councilor Knight.

237
00:21:21,982 --> 00:21:25,485
[SPEAKER_03]: Don't we have a noise ordinance already that would address the constant droning and nuisance ordinance?

238
00:21:29,969 --> 00:21:32,131
[SPEAKER_01]: I will check on that.

239
00:21:33,112 --> 00:21:42,020
[SPEAKER_05]: From my reading of the committee report, I didn't capture that Commissioner Moki felt that that would be something they could enforce, but yeah.

240
00:21:42,447 --> 00:21:44,428
[SPEAKER_05]: but that's just my reading of it.

241
00:21:44,749 --> 00:21:48,171
[SPEAKER_05]: If it is, then I don't think it would need to be included in this ordinance.

242
00:21:49,292 --> 00:21:50,212
[SPEAKER_03]: I just referenced it, you know?

243
00:21:51,193 --> 00:21:51,413
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

244
00:21:55,696 --> 00:21:59,299
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a second for Councilor, Vice-President Bear's motion?

245
00:22:00,399 --> 00:22:08,065
[SPEAKER_05]: And before I, given Councilor Nye's point, I'll amend the motion to say either draft language pending

246
00:22:10,128 --> 00:22:14,130
[SPEAKER_05]: request to the building commissioner as to whether the noise ordinance would already cover that.

247
00:22:14,910 --> 00:22:18,092
[SPEAKER_03]: We got a lawyer here we're paying $65,000 so we can have them research it.

248
00:22:18,192 --> 00:22:20,913
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh yeah, yeah, sure.

249
00:22:20,993 --> 00:22:22,214
[SPEAKER_05]: That makes that makes sense.

250
00:22:22,234 --> 00:22:22,874
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm fine with that.

251
00:22:23,135 --> 00:22:30,238
[SPEAKER_05]: We'll ask KP a lot of just see if this already is covered in the noise ordinance and if not, then to draft language to include it in this ordinance.

252
00:22:37,540 --> 00:22:40,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, do I have a second on a vices and bears motion.

253
00:22:40,942 --> 00:22:45,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Second, because they're saying, and you had more vices and bears.

254
00:22:46,446 --> 00:22:51,809
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, I have some serious problems about section B three.

255
00:22:54,191 --> 00:23:02,596
[SPEAKER_05]: At the very least, I think we should update the definition of commercially floor to include those single operators as was previously mentioned.

256
00:23:05,445 --> 00:23:12,773
[SPEAKER_05]: And I think at the end of the day, most of the issues that folks have around this are with commercial operators.

257
00:23:13,954 --> 00:23:24,124
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't see residents out every week for multiple hours a day with multiple leaf blowers, creating lots of noise and pollution in our neighborhoods.

258
00:23:24,204 --> 00:23:27,547
[SPEAKER_05]: But I do think that's what the commercial operators are doing.

259
00:23:29,063 --> 00:23:46,557
[SPEAKER_05]: So I don't have a motion on this right now but I'm personally reticent to create a separate set of standards for people who are doing this for profit and have an incentive to minimize the time that they spend

260
00:23:48,253 --> 00:23:53,756
[SPEAKER_05]: cleaning a property and maximize the revenue that they're gaining from it.

261
00:23:54,737 --> 00:23:58,118
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that sets them apart from a resident maintaining their own property.

262
00:23:58,138 --> 00:24:03,401
[SPEAKER_05]: I think that sets them apart from our DPW maintaining public property.

263
00:24:03,541 --> 00:24:06,483
[SPEAKER_05]: So I'm just putting that out there for further discussion.

264
00:24:08,624 --> 00:24:10,065
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, I think we'll be coming back

265
00:24:11,456 --> 00:24:12,517
[SPEAKER_05]: and discussing this again.

266
00:24:12,537 --> 00:24:21,608
[SPEAKER_05]: And I may make a motion at a later time, but maybe that's something to discuss after we have some commercial operators invited.

267
00:24:21,628 --> 00:24:24,712
[SPEAKER_05]: But I just want to put that out into a discussion.

268
00:24:25,013 --> 00:24:26,134
[SPEAKER_05]: So that's all I have.

269
00:24:26,214 --> 00:24:26,635
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you.

270
00:24:26,655 --> 00:24:28,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

271
00:24:28,417 --> 00:24:30,259
[SPEAKER_01]: I would agree with you there because it does seem like

272
00:24:30,746 --> 00:24:39,333
[SPEAKER_01]: In my opinion, we're putting this effort into these regulations and that number three, B3 kind of invalidates all of them if you submit a plan.

273
00:24:39,353 --> 00:24:41,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think that's great.

274
00:24:41,435 --> 00:24:47,279
[SPEAKER_01]: I think these plans, I think the requirements of the plan are important, but that's not what residents are asking us.

275
00:24:47,339 --> 00:24:55,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Residents are asking us for relief from the constant noise, the constant emissions, the constant dust and debris flying into their yards and their windows.

276
00:24:56,687 --> 00:24:57,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So I would agree with you there.

277
00:24:59,904 --> 00:25:00,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Councilor Collins.

278
00:25:01,765 --> 00:25:02,645
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you, President Merrill.

279
00:25:03,005 --> 00:25:13,289
[SPEAKER_02]: I just wanted to add my voice in a sense, you know, I think with regulations like these I do think it's important to provide for opportunities for exceptions, exemptions, where they're appropriate.

280
00:25:13,729 --> 00:25:22,312
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, I think it's a great idea to invite the Chamber of Commerce and commercial leaf blower operators to a next meeting on this because I think it would be helpful if we could

281
00:25:22,908 --> 00:25:28,789
[SPEAKER_02]: maybe amend this section to be a little more targeted for what those potential exceptions and exemptions could be.

282
00:25:28,829 --> 00:25:30,329
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that you stated it well.

283
00:25:30,369 --> 00:25:43,652
[SPEAKER_02]: I think this is kind of what I was thinking about in the question earlier is if we devise stricter regulations and then there's a permit that waives all of them, that's a pretty big wave.

284
00:25:44,272 --> 00:25:48,413
[SPEAKER_02]: So I think that I too would be interested in discussing which

285
00:25:50,993 --> 00:25:59,440
[SPEAKER_02]: which regulations could be important to have more wiggle room on and where maybe we could tighten this to just keep it in the spirit of the ordinance.

286
00:26:00,340 --> 00:26:00,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Thank you.

287
00:26:01,541 --> 00:26:02,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Councilor Collins.

288
00:26:04,283 --> 00:26:06,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Any further discussion from Councillors?

289
00:26:08,827 --> 00:26:11,829
[SPEAKER_01]: I do have a few questions, Commissioner McGivern.

290
00:26:12,810 --> 00:26:19,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there intent or plans or has there been just kind of fact finding around what it would cost and if

291
00:26:20,117 --> 00:26:28,641
[SPEAKER_01]: the work could be accomplished if the city were to transition to battery or electric leaf blowers, which don't have the emissions and they tend to have much less noise as well.

292
00:26:31,462 --> 00:26:35,184
[SPEAKER_04]: Not that I'm aware of, but I can find out if something has been done in the past.

293
00:26:35,484 --> 00:26:38,806
[SPEAKER_04]: We also have contractors that would use leaf blowers.

294
00:26:39,446 --> 00:26:45,049
[SPEAKER_04]: I believe industry sort of preference is still gas, you know, fuel powered.

295
00:26:46,905 --> 00:26:53,068
[SPEAKER_04]: But yeah, I can find out if any work has been done and not that I am aware of and not since I've been at the helm.

296
00:26:54,408 --> 00:26:55,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

297
00:26:55,649 --> 00:27:02,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And then also, at what regularity are our crews using leaf blowers on these parcels?

298
00:27:03,151 --> 00:27:03,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it weekly?

299
00:27:03,652 --> 00:27:04,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it monthly?

300
00:27:04,232 --> 00:27:08,993
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I know, you know, I walk in the cemetery a lot and go to the parks and they look pretty well clean.

301
00:27:09,074 --> 00:27:12,955
[SPEAKER_01]: So I know they're working when I'm not seeing them, but I'm just curious how often.

302
00:27:14,445 --> 00:27:16,387
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, I'm pretty sure it's as needed.

303
00:27:16,407 --> 00:27:19,249
[SPEAKER_04]: So we're definitely not out there every day.

304
00:27:19,549 --> 00:27:30,217
[SPEAKER_04]: So you would probably notice in the fall, you know, probably weekly during, you know, the, the, the high fully or the high falling, you know, time period.

305
00:27:31,999 --> 00:27:36,082
[SPEAKER_04]: But, you know, we're not, we're not going to be using leaf blowers if there's nothing to clean up.

306
00:27:36,702 --> 00:27:44,048
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but I do know it's, it's common practice during those high intensity use time periods that, you know, we're pretty much nailed down there in the,

307
00:27:45,195 --> 00:27:46,135
[SPEAKER_04]: in the language there, but.

308
00:27:48,356 --> 00:27:48,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Great, thank you.

309
00:27:49,056 --> 00:27:56,578
[SPEAKER_01]: And then this gets pun intended a little into the weeds, but I know some of the frustration residents have is the leaf blowers are used.

310
00:27:57,058 --> 00:28:01,059
[SPEAKER_01]: They're using them to blow around lawn clippings or a little bit of dust in a driveway.

311
00:28:01,079 --> 00:28:06,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Does the city, you know, truly use leaf blowers for blowing leaves and those cleanup times?

312
00:28:06,601 --> 00:28:09,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are they used for, you know, mowing projects or other things?

313
00:28:13,541 --> 00:28:19,889
[SPEAKER_04]: They are good tools for general cleanup of anything like grass clippings on the ground that you can blow.

314
00:28:19,909 --> 00:28:23,312
[SPEAKER_04]: They're used for cleanup of anything like that, I would imagine.

315
00:28:23,372 --> 00:28:27,657
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't know for sure, but I can just imagine just from my own experience and seeing crews out there.

316
00:28:27,757 --> 00:28:34,165
[SPEAKER_04]: But primarily leaves and grass clippings, things like that.

317
00:28:35,215 --> 00:28:35,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, great.

318
00:28:35,916 --> 00:28:36,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you.

319
00:28:36,337 --> 00:28:47,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And last question for me, and I know you're new into this role, but to your knowledge, does DPW get complaints about the use of leaf blowers in these larger, you know, in the parks and these larger parcels?

320
00:28:47,312 --> 00:28:50,357
[SPEAKER_01]: I know this stuff tends to come from just dense neighborhoods.

321
00:28:51,972 --> 00:28:53,393
[SPEAKER_04]: Right, I don't know the answer.

322
00:28:53,413 --> 00:29:04,984
[SPEAKER_04]: I haven't experienced any complaints about leaf blowers directly to me or even really through the office, but I would imagine every now and then we get a complaint about something like that.

323
00:29:05,024 --> 00:29:08,487
[SPEAKER_04]: It seems to me like one of those things that we would get complaints on at the DPW.

324
00:29:09,488 --> 00:29:16,994
[SPEAKER_04]: I can kind of follow up and see, you know, take the temperature there on what kind of complaint level we see.

325
00:29:18,213 --> 00:29:36,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and I think yeah to count some nights points I think you know, holding the city to to a similar high standard I've absolutely like just, you know, looking into that and I think, like I said, I think most of us get complaints just from the neighborhood aspect, or I haven't gotten complaints regarding larger parcel parcels but it would be really helpful to have that information as we move forward.

326
00:29:36,878 --> 00:29:40,359
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you mind if I make a comment or two.

327
00:29:42,392 --> 00:29:48,397
[SPEAKER_04]: So I just noticed while you're passing through the ordinances that you had a bunch of parks listed there.

328
00:29:48,417 --> 00:30:02,447
[SPEAKER_04]: And I would say, if we're going the direction of, you know, keeping this commercial in excluding city properties make sure we're including city buildings and the grounds around city buildings, as well as schools, which would be included to build this.

329
00:30:03,087 --> 00:30:04,148
[SPEAKER_04]: They're just bigger pieces of property.

330
00:30:05,029 --> 00:30:05,649
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe you already did that.

331
00:30:05,669 --> 00:30:07,230
[SPEAKER_04]: I just didn't see them in the list.

332
00:30:07,991 --> 00:30:25,284
[SPEAKER_04]: And then I think if you were to go to the other direction and hold the city to this standard too, I would definitely want to take a closer look, be a little bit more involved, and definitely give the folks that are doing this sort of work on the ground an opportunity to comment and weigh in on this.

333
00:30:26,225 --> 00:30:31,830
[SPEAKER_04]: So the lead of our park division, for example, Mike Nestor, he would be an excellent resource

334
00:30:32,541 --> 00:30:35,064
[SPEAKER_04]: to help you guys out, for example.

335
00:30:35,724 --> 00:30:44,853
[SPEAKER_04]: So I just want to put that on your radar if it does go in that direction, because we would need to, we would want to inform you and then we would want to react and plan appropriately.

336
00:30:46,034 --> 00:30:46,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

337
00:30:46,575 --> 00:30:47,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Commissioner McIver.

338
00:30:48,437 --> 00:30:49,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Vice President Behrs.

339
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:52,141
[SPEAKER_05]: Thank you, Madam President.

340
00:30:54,103 --> 00:30:56,185
[SPEAKER_05]: I would first happily motion that

341
00:30:57,252 --> 00:31:13,576
[SPEAKER_05]: we amend the language that currently lists a number of city properties, just to say all city properties and grounds or whatever phrasing the DPW commissioner thinks would best include all city properties.

342
00:31:16,897 --> 00:31:18,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I have a second on vice-president Bear's motion?

343
00:31:21,298 --> 00:31:22,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Councillor Collins on the second.

344
00:31:27,914 --> 00:31:31,375
[SPEAKER_05]: And then I actually have a question for the commissioner.

345
00:31:33,016 --> 00:31:45,320
[SPEAKER_05]: When city employees are contract, well, maybe you don't have as much control over contractors, but at least when city employees are using leaf blowers, do they wear hearing or respiratory protection?

346
00:31:49,861 --> 00:31:50,142
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

347
00:31:50,302 --> 00:31:57,024
[SPEAKER_04]: So if tools or equipment are, you know, have a risk of causing ear damage,

348
00:31:57,837 --> 00:32:04,662
[SPEAKER_04]: then, you know, ear protection is worn or eye, you know, same with eye protection, same with, you know, wearing safety vests.

349
00:32:04,702 --> 00:32:09,005
[SPEAKER_04]: So PPE, we try to make sure that we're using appropriate PPE.

350
00:32:10,527 --> 00:32:25,678
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, I would move that the ordinance require that commercial operators and operators on the large parcels OLP be required to

351
00:32:26,497 --> 00:32:29,598
[SPEAKER_05]: provide effective PPE for hearing.

352
00:32:31,058 --> 00:32:32,859
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, just let's just say effective PPE.

353
00:32:32,919 --> 00:32:37,540
[SPEAKER_05]: And if we need to itemize that we can for their workers.

